---
title: Session Quality Hooks
description: Tune the session-insights runner from session-quality-hooks.ts, a file pika sync never overwrites
---

The session-insights runner analyzes finished chat sessions on a schedule. Which sessions it analyzes, who is told about a flagged session, and what counts as a tool failure vary by deployment, so the runner reads those decisions from hooks instead of hard-coding them. Pika ships the hooks with defaults that keep the runner's standard behavior, and you override only what you need without patching the runner.

## Where the hooks live

| File | Owner | What it holds |
|------|-------|---------------|
| `services/pika/src/lib/session-quality-base.ts` | Pika (sync updates it) | The hook types and `defaultSessionQualityHooks` |
| `services/pika/src/lib/custom/session-quality-hooks.ts` | You (sync never touches it) | Only the members you want to override |

`services/pika/src/lib/custom/**` is protected by the default protected-area list of the installed `pika-app` CLI (1.9.0 and later). `services/pika/test/custom/**` is protected the same way.

{% aside type="caution" title="Upgrade pika-cli before you sync" %}
Sync uses the protected list of the installed CLI. With a `pika-app` older than 1.9.0, `pika sync` overwrites `session-quality-hooks.ts`. Upgrade the CLI first, or add both paths to `userProtectedAreas` in `.pika-sync.json`.
{% /aside %}

Your file exports `sessionQualityHooks`, a `Partial<SessionQualityHooks>`. Pika resolves the hooks at call time: a member you define replaces the default, and a member you leave out keeps it. Setting a member to `undefined` also keeps the default, except `toolFailureBodySentinel`, where `undefined` turns the check off.

## Members

| Member | Default | Called |
|--------|---------|--------|
| `isSweepEnabled()` | `true` | At the start of each scheduled sweep. `false` skips the sweep. |
| `getSweepStartFloor()` | `undefined` | Once per sweep. Returns an ISO date; the sweep skips sessions last active before it. `undefined` defers to the next source (see [Start floor](#start-floor)). |
| `getChatAppAllowlist()` | `undefined` | Once per sweep. A `Set` of chat app ids to analyze. `undefined` means every chat app, and an empty set means none. |
| `requiresEmployeeFollowup(assessments)` | critical issues present, follow-up required, or escalation needed | For each analyzed session. `true` marks the session as urgent. |
| `buildFlaggedSessionNotification(event)` | `undefined` (no notification) | For an urgent session that has new messages since its last analysis. Returns an object with a `send()` method, or `undefined`. |
| `shouldMarkForInsightsOnThumbsDown(ctx)` | `false` | When an end user or an admin saves a thumbs-down on a session that isn't already queued. `true` queues the session for analysis. |
| `toolFailureBodySentinel` | `undefined` | A string. A tool response body that starts with it counts as a tool failure in the insights payload. |

Set the sentinel in both custom_hooks.py and session-quality-hooks.ts; each side reads only its own.

`buildFlaggedSessionNotification` receives a `FlaggedSessionEvent` with `session`, `scoring`, `urgent` and `notes`. Pika calls `send()` only after the session's analysis update is saved. If a page is retried, the session's earlier pending update and notification are replaced, so each flagged session produces one notification.

Notifications are at-most-once. If the Lambda times out or `send()` throws after the update is saved, that notification is not retried. The `INSIGHTS_FLAGGED` log line is written for every flagged session, so a CloudWatch metric filter on it is the durable record.

A session whose chat app isn't in `getChatAppAllowlist` has its `insight_status` cleared without analysis, so it isn't re-read on every sweep. Widening the allowlist later covers only sessions with new activity after the change.

`shouldMarkForInsightsOnThumbsDown` receives a `ThumbsDownContext` with `userId`, `sessionId`, `chatAppId`, `sessionAttributes` and `source` (`'end-user'` or `'admin'`). When the member isn't overridden, a thumbs-down does no extra reads.

## When a hook throws

Every call is wrapped. A throw is logged as `[SESSION-QUALITY] <hook> threw` and handled per hook:

| Hook | On a throw |
|------|-----------|
| `isSweepEnabled` | The sweep is skipped |
| `getSweepStartFloor` | The sweep is skipped; no floor stamped |
| `getChatAppAllowlist` | An empty set, so the sweep is skipped |
| `requiresEmployeeFollowup` | The default predicate |
| `buildFlaggedSessionNotification` | No notification |
| `shouldMarkForInsightsOnThumbsDown` | `false`, and the feedback is still saved |

A broken enable flag or allowlist stops the sweep instead of spending Bedrock on sessions you meant to exclude.

## Start floor

The runner picks its start floor from the first of these that is set:

1. The `getSweepStartFloor` hook.
2. The `INSIGHTS_START_FLOOR` environment variable, which the `sessionInsights.startFloor` construct prop sets.
3. A stamp. On the first scheduled sweep, the runner writes the current time as an item named `session-insights-runner#start-floor` in the runner mutex table, then reuses it on every later sweep. The item has no expiry.

On the upgrade that introduces the runner's start floor, the first sweep stamps "now", so a backlog of sessions queued earlier isn't analyzed. To backfill, set `sessionInsights.startFloor` to an ISO date in `getPikaConstructProps` (in `custom-stack-defs.ts`) before the first sweep, or delete the stamp item from the mutex table. A single-session invoke (`{ sessionId, userId }`) ignores the floor and the sweep gates but takes the sweep lock; while a sweep holds it, the invoke returns `{ busy: true }` without analyzing. If the stamp can't be read or written, the sweep is skipped.

## Example

```typescript
// services/pika/src/lib/custom/session-quality-hooks.ts
import type { SessionQualityHooks } from '../session-quality-base';

export const sessionQualityHooks: Partial<SessionQualityHooks> = {
    getChatAppAllowlist: async () => new Set(['example-app']),

    buildFlaggedSessionNotification: async ({ session, urgent }) => ({
        send: async () => {
            await fetch('https://example.com/hooks', {
                method: 'POST',
                headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                body: JSON.stringify({ sessionId: session.sessionId, urgent })
            });
        }
    })
};
```

## Testing

Pika's jest config maps every import whose path ends in `/custom/session-quality-hooks` to an empty default, `services/pika/test/setup/default-session-quality-hooks.ts`. Pika's own tests therefore run against the defaults even when your file overrides them, and a test that imports `session-quality-hooks.ts` itself also gets the empty default. Keep your hook logic in its own modules under `services/pika/src/lib/custom/`, import them from `session-quality-hooks.ts`, and put their tests in `services/pika/test/custom/`.
